    ":question/math-academy-id": "e-3381",
    ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $f (x, y) = \\ln (x^2y)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2, 1)?$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\\nabla f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\u2207f(x,y) & =-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}y))\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}y))\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot 2xy\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}\\cdot {x}^{2}j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{1}{y}\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\ -\u2207f(2,1) & =-\\frac{2}{2}\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{1}{1}\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $- \\, \\mathbf i - \\mathbf j.$"
  },
