      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-36293",
      ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+{x}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $\u2207f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{\u00a0}i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+{x}^{2}y)\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =(2xy-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)\\text{\u00a0}i+{x}^{2}\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\ \u2207f(1,1) & =(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(1))\\text{\u00a0}i+{1}^{2}\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =(2-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(1))\\text{\u00a0}i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $(2-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(1))\\,i+j.$"
    },
