      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-83598",
      ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+z)$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\u2207g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\u2207g(x,y,z) & =-\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+z)),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+z)),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+z))\u27e9 \\\\  & =-\u27e8{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x+z},\\,2xy,\\,-\\frac{4}{x+z}\u27e9 \\\\ -\u2207g(1,1,1) & =-\u27e8{1}^{2}-\\frac{4}{1+1},\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{4}{1+1}\u27e9 \\\\  & =-\u27e8-1,\\,2,\\,-2\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81,\\,-2,\\,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $\u27e81,\\,-2,\\,2\u27e9.$"
    },
