      ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,2,-1)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $\u2207g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207g(x,y,z) & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8y{e}^{xy},\\,x{e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2},\\,6yz\u27e9 \\\\ \u2207g(2,2,-1) & =\u27e82\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2},\\,2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2}+3(-1)^{2},\\,6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $\u27e82{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12\u27e9.$"
    },
