      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-83562",
      ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+{x}^{2}y$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(\\pi ,0)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-\u2207f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\pi ,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\u2207f(x,y) & =-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{\u00a0}i-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+2xy)\\text{\u00a0}i-(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y+{x}^{2})\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\ -\u2207f(\\pi ,0) & =-(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi \\operatorname{cos}\u20610+2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot 0)\\text{\u00a0}i+(-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi \\operatorname{sin}\u20610+{\\pi }^{2})\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j.$"
    },
