      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-130279",
      ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)={e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({x}^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $\u2207f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({x}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({x}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =2x{e}^{2y}{sec}^{2}\u2061({x}^{2})\\,i+2{e}^{2y}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061({x}^{2})\\,j \\\\ \u2207f(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2},0) & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}){e}^{0}{sec}^{2}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $2\\sqrt{\\pi }\\,i+2\\,j.$"
    },
