      ":question/id": "6d63b608-6564-54eb-94c2-8afc07416275",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-86540",
      ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-y)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,-1,0)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $\u2207g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207g(x,y,z) & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-y)),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-y)),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x-y))\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,-\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,8z\u27e9 \\\\ \u2207g(2,-1,0) & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,-\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,8\\cdot 0\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $\u27e8\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0\u27e9.$"
