      ":question/id": "f39e417c-6152-5bd7-a05f-5fd83a9d6cdc",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-130272",
      ":question/problem": "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1,2)?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $\u2207g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207g(x,y,z) & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{z}^{2},\\,-3{y}^{2},\\,2xz\u27e9 \\\\ \u2207g(0,1,2) & =\u27e8{2}^{2},-3(1)^{2},2(0)(2)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84,-3,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $\u27e84,-3,0\u27e9.$"
